  Celery Notes
    So what celery does is it allows a python function to run many times
      in parallel by running that function inside a "worker". Once a
      function is defined, you have to run the celery application using
      the celery binary. When the binary process is running, the function
      where the worker is defined can be imported into other python processes,
      and those processes can kickoff parallel procedures simply by
      calling the imported function - provided the celery binary is running.
    I honestly don't like this, because it means we're interfacing with
      a task queue that operates on a separate process through python imports.
      I was thinking I would be able to setup celery workers on one server,
      and then call them from another server. It doesn't seem like that's
      a standard use-case based on the documentation.
    In fact, it seems like in order to use Celery at all, I have to run two
      separate processes inside the same system - the Celery process that
      runs the workers, and a separate python process (ipython, a Django app, etc)
      that actually calls the workers.
    That means if I want to do this with Docker I'm going to have to write a
      startup script that starts both celery and whatever module actually kicks
      off the celery tasks

